---
import LabAssessment from "../../components/LabAssessment.astro";
import { EXERCICIOS_DA_AMOSTRAGEM, MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_AMOSTRAGEM } from "@ovh/otel-domain";
import { AmostragemLab } from "../../components/AmostragemLab.tsx";
import { Exercicio } from "../../components/Exercicio.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/AmostragemLab.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
import "../../components/PainelDaCarga.css";
import "@ovh/depth-ui/src/stage.css";
import "../../components/Explorer.css";
import "../../components/Exercicio.css";
import { montarExercicio } from "../../lib/exercicios";

const exercicios = EXERCICIOS_DA_AMOSTRAGEM.map(montarExercicio);
---

<Base
  domain="otel"
  title="Head vs tail sampling — OpenLabs"
  description="Head sampling decides at the root span, before the request has failed. Tail sampling decides on the finished trace, and so it has to hold every span in memory — and it is only right if every span of a trace reaches the same instance."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel")}>OpenTelemetry Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>The trace you needed was the one you threw away</h1>
        <p>
          Sampling is usually discussed as a percentage, which hides the only
          thing that matters about it: <b>when</b> the decision is taken. A head
          sampler decides at the root span, before the request has done
          anything — so it cannot keep &ldquo;the traces that failed&rdquo;,
          because nothing has failed yet. A tail sampler decides on the finished
          trace and can, at the price of holding every span of every trace in
          memory until it decides.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below, each trace has a few spans across two services, and the error
          is always in the <em>last</em> one. Turn the head sampler up and watch
          the counter of <em>error traces cut</em> climb: those are gone, and no
          tail policy can bring them back, because they never arrived. Then turn
          the load balancer's trace routing off.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>One tick is one second.</p>
        <p>The sampling rate is a hash of the trace id, like the real sampler.</p>
        <p>Nothing here is scripted: every number is the run.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-palco">
      <AmostragemLab client:only="react" />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <p>Three moves. The third one is a production incident that reports nothing.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Head sampler to 1 in 10, tail keeping everything.</b> The
            <em>cut before the error existed</em> counter climbs, and
            <em>error traces · whole</em> stops growing. This is the whole
            argument against head sampling as a reliability tool: the decision
            could not depend on the error, so what you keep is a slice of
            traffic and not a slice of each category. A failure in 1 in 500
            requests, sampled at 10%, is a failure you meet once in 5 000.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Head sampler back to 1 in 1, tail keeping 1 in 10 of the rest.</b>
            Now every error trace survives — <em>kept · error</em> equals the
            number that failed — and the boring traffic is thinned out. Then
            raise the decision window and watch <em>holding</em> in both
            gateways: that is memory, it belongs to traces that are about to be
            discarded, and it is the price of deciding late.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Turn off <code>load balancer routes by traceID</code>.</b> Nothing
            looks broken. The gateways keep deciding, the backend keeps filling,
            and <em>arrived broken</em> starts climbing: traces that are in the
            backend without the span that failed. Each gateway decided correctly
            on the fragment it received, and the fragment was not a trace. In
            production nothing reports this, because no backend knows how many
            spans a trace had.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>
          Answer first, then run it. Predicting and being wrong is what makes
          the answer stick.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="Your SDKs sample at 10% (head). Your Collector runs tail sampling with a policy that keeps every trace containing an error. What fraction of error traces reaches the backend?"
          opcoes={[
            "About 10% — the head sampler already threw the rest away before the Collector saw them",
            "100% — the tail policy keeps every error trace it decides on",
            "About 10%, but the tail policy can be configured to recover the rest",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="Tail sampling can only decide about spans that reach it. Head sampling happens first, in the SDK, and what it drops is never exported — so the tail policy is applied to the 10% that survived, and it keeps every error inside that 10%. This is why the recommendation when you run tail sampling is to leave the SDK sampler on AlwaysOn: two samplers in series multiply, and the first one is the one that cannot know anything."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Configure it</h2>
        <p>
          Three lines from <code>labs/head-vs-tail-sampling/</code>, which is
          this model as something that boots. The correct block of each one is
          lifted out of the file that runs, so it cannot drift from the config.
        </p>
        <p>
          They are in <b>three different files</b>, and that is the point rather
          than variety: the sampling decision is taken in three places owned by
          three different people. The SDK sampler belongs to whoever writes the
          application, <code>routing_key</code> to whoever operates the cluster,
          and the policies to whoever looks after observability. None of the
          three sees the other two files, and each of them can, alone, delete the
          trace of the error.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-exercicios">
        {exercicios.map((exercicio) => (
          <Exercicio client:visible exercicio={exercicio} />
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people get wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Every row below is a belief worth naming, because the model above is
          arranged to take it apart. The right column is the documentation, with
          the link.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-mal-entendidos">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What people believe</th>
              <th scope="col">What the docs say</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_AMOSTRAGEM.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{m.crenca}</td>
                  <td>
                    {m.spec}{" "}
                    <a href={m.fonte} rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">
                      source
                    </a>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What this model does not claim</h2>
        <p>
          Two simplifications, declared rather than hidden. First, the sampling
          here is <b>deterministic</b>: the decision is a hash of the trace id,
          reproducible across runs, so two regimes can be compared instead of
          two draws. Real head sampling hashes the trace id too — that is why
          its rate is approximate — but it is a rate and not a schedule.
        </p>
        <p>
          Second, all spans of a trace leave the application in the same tick.
          In a real system they are produced over the life of the request, which
          is the actual reason a tail sampler needs a decision window at all.
          The window here is real and it is a parameter, but what it waits for
          is the model's wiring and not the duration of a request.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <LabAssessment href="labs/head-vs-tail-sampling" />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .lab-palco {
    margin-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos th,
  .lab-mal-entendidos td {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
